  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
        <lg xml:id="TNV21">
        <l>mukkaṭ kūṭṭa mutalā nāṉkun</l>
<l>tokkiya loḻukkaṅ kaṟpeṉat tōṉṟum.</l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en"><l>All the four [situations], which have as their basis the union based on three [things],<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mukkaṭ kūṭṭam</hi></q> has troubled scholars for generations, and I am not confident that it is yet fully understood. The most commonly accepted meaning of the phrase is that this refers to the unions that occur by means of three things – fate, the hero’s companion, and the heroine’s companion. I am not convinced. For a detailed discussion on this topic, see \S\ref{sec:mukkat} of the introduction.</note></l> 
  <l>appear together as the natural conduct called <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaṟpu</hi></term>.</l></lg>
</div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV21comm1">eṉpatu kaṟpām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
      <p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which the public phase occurs.</p>
 </div>
 <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
      <p xml:id="TNV21comm2">aṟattoṭu nilaiyum uṭaṉ celavuñ cēy iṭaip pirivum āy iṭaip pirivum 
 eṉap paṭṭa nāṉkun tokka voḻukka muk kaṭ kūṭṭam uṭaiya kaṟpeṉṟu ciṟappikkap paṭum eṉṟa vāṟu.<note> 21.</note></p>
 <p xml:lang="en">The conduct that is all the four [situations] together, namely: standing with duty; the elopement; the long-distance separation; and the separation to ‘that place’ – has the special designation <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kaṟpu</hi></term>, which possesses the union based on three [things].</p>
</div>
</body>
 </text>
